WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.860
Michael Keaton almost went "beyond"&nbsp;
"Batman Returns" with another solo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.860
outing as the caped crusader. So what went wrong?

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:13.860
The year was 1989, and Warner Bros. was more than&nbsp;
ready to give Batman another trip to the movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.780
Unlike the previous incarnation, brought&nbsp;
to life by the late great Adam West,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:22.920
this version would be much&nbsp;
darker, serious, and mysterious.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.340
Michael Keaton was selected to&nbsp;
portray the "Caped Crusader,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:31.200
going one-on-one against Jack Nicholson's&nbsp;
Joker under the direction of Tim Burton.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.880
Simply titled "Batman," this feature would&nbsp;
revolutionize the comic book movie genre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.920
rake in mountains of cash for all involved, and&nbsp;
prove that there was more to Keaton than comedy.

00:00:41.034 --> 00:00:44.534
"You wanna get nuts!? COME ON! Let's get nuts!"

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.900
Due to the success of "Batman," Keaton&nbsp;
got to don the hard rubber cowl once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:54.180
again in 1992 via "Batman Returns" —&nbsp;
a controversial sequel that derailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:58.980
Burton's time as a Batman director and&nbsp;
served as Keaton's unexpected swan song.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:04.260
In the decades that followed, Keaton never shied&nbsp;
away from reminding folks that he is and forever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:09.120
will be Batman, but it seemed unlikely he'd&nbsp;
ever get to take on the role again. That is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.280
until it came to light in the early years of the&nbsp;
2020s that he'd reprise his role in several DC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:19.920
features, such as "The Flash," "Aquaman and the&nbsp;
Lost Kingdom," and the now-canceled "Batgirl."

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.460
As it turns out, these cameos&nbsp;
weren't all Michael Keaton's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.480
rendition of Batman would have to sink&nbsp;
his teeth into. He was also reportedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:31.440
in line for a movie all his own that&nbsp;
has been unceremoniously scrapped.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:37.020
On December 8, 2022, John Rocha and Jeff&nbsp;
Sneider streamed an episode of their show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:42.420
"The Hot Mic," where Sneider dropped an intriguing&nbsp;
tidbit regarding the plan for Michael Keaton's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:47.040
Batman. According to his sources, plans were&nbsp;
in place for him to get his own solo film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:53.760
but the powers that be — DC Studios heads James&nbsp;
Gunn and Peter Safran, President and CEO of Warner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:59.220
Bros. Discovery David Zaslav, or someone else&nbsp;
entirely — elected to wipe it from the release&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:04.020
slate. Sneider also claims that Christina Hodson&nbsp;
was the one tasked with writing the project.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:08.940
Other than these few morsels of information,&nbsp;
Sneider didn't have anything else to offer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:13.252
regarding this production. Thankfully, it didn't&nbsp;
take long for the plot to thicken elsewhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.252 --> 00:02:16.500
Umberto Gonzalez of TheWrap&nbsp;
took to Twitter to add another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.540
eyebrow-raising detail to this canceled&nbsp;
Keaton Batman movie situation, posting,

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.320
This means fans almost saw a&nbsp;
live-action adaptation of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.480
beloved DC animated series,&nbsp;
which ran from 1999 to 2001.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:44.460
"Batman Beyond" has fans the world over who would&nbsp;
surely love to see some of its biggest story beats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:49.320
replicated at the movies. Michael Keaton more&nbsp;
than has what it takes to play the secluded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:54.120
elderly version of Bruce Wayne we see on the&nbsp;
show, as voiced by the irreplaceable Kevin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:59.640
Conroy. Sadly, if these reports are indeed true&nbsp;
and DC Studios is no longer working on a "Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:05.040
Beyond" movie featuring Keaton, we'll likely&nbsp;
never get to see him try. Oddly enough, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.900
isn't the first time rumblings of such a film made&nbsp;
the rounds only to end up in the bin in the end.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:15.600
Way back in 2000, Boaz Yakin was selected&nbsp;
to co-write and direct a live-action "Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.920
Beyond" movie. The minds behind the&nbsp;
TV show, Paul Dini and Alan Burnett,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:25.740
signed on to write the screenplay, which they&nbsp;
completed and submitted in 2001. Ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:30.840
as we now know, in 2022, Warner Bros. didn't give&nbsp;
the film the green light to enter production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.000
so it wound up trashed. It is worth noting&nbsp;
that, for this feature, Keaton wasn't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.400
considered to play the aged Bruce Wayne. Yakin&nbsp;
actually wanted to see cinematic icon Clint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:45.900
Eastwood take on the part, adding his name&nbsp;
to the pantheon of live-action Batman actors.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:50.880
Not only is it disappointing that another supposed&nbsp;
attempt at bringing "Batman Beyond" to the silver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.740
screen didn't pan out, but in this instance, we&nbsp;
were apparently pretty close to seeing Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:00.600
Keaton's Bruce Wayne mentor Terry McGinnis.&nbsp;
Maybe someday this endeavor or one like it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:06.660
will become a reality, but on DC Studios' current&nbsp;
roadmap, the odds certainly aren't in its favor.

